/**
 * The non-termination effect: a computation that may run forever.
 *
 * Eliot's core cannot express recursion or loops, so every program terminates by default; `Inf` is the
 * one opt-out. A computation that loops endlessly declares `uses Inf` and calls `forever`; the effect
 * propagates to callers like any other, so using `forever` without declaring `uses Inf` is a compile
 * error. Unlike an interpretation effect, `Inf` is never discharged — it is *run*. An `Inf` reaching
 * `main` is therefore allowed: it denotes a deliberately non-terminating program, such as a server or
 * firmware super-loop, and the platform's own implementation is what the entry point binds.
 *
 * ```eliot
 * def processRequestsForever uses Dep[RequestSource], Inf: Unit =
 *   forever {
 *     dependency.readRequest.process
 *   }
 * ```
 */
effect Inf {
   /**
    * Run `step` endlessly, never returning normally. Each iteration re-runs `step`, so its effects
    * repeat; this is the only sanctioned source of divergence in a program.
    *
    * `step` is a suspended slot supplying nothing, so it performs exactly what the enclosing definition
    * declares, on the bindings it was written under.
    */
   def forever(step uses *: Unit): Unit
}
